README.OSK -- disp, "Writing a device driver in C"The example from OS-9 International issue 1/93, page 35: an SCF devicedriver written in C, with scf_c.a as the assembler glue that turns SCF'scalls into C calls.  It drives a four-bit display latch at one portaddress: Write puts the low four bits of each character on the port andRead returns the low four bits read back.  di is its descriptor, with theport set to $FEC30000; change that for your own hardware before use.CMDS/DRIVERS/disp and CMDS/DRIVERS/di are the code disk's own builds (themakefile uses gcc2).Changes: none.Not tested.  The emulator loads no drivers; this is an example to read andadapt more than a program to install as it is.